<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.4.1: Finding the Solution to an Equation in One Variable, Part 1</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00109</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.4.1: Finding the Solution to an Equation in One Variable, Part 1</md:title>
    <md:uuid>42cb8bfe-3c19-4792-9cd7-e60a88a93370</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <!--Q1 -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">A granola bite contains 27 calories. Most of the calories come from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> grams of carbohydrates. The rest come from other ingredients. One gram of carbohydrate contains 4 calories.</para>
          <para id="para-00002">The equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>27</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> represents the relationship between these quantities.</para>
          <para id="para-00003">What could the 5 represent in this situation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">It could represent the calories from other ingredients in the granola bite.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q2 -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Priya said that neither 8 nor 3 could be the solution to the equation. Is she correct?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Yes, she is correct that 8 and 3 could not be solutions. Sample explanation: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is 37, not 27, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is 17, not 27.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Marcus said the solution is 5.5. Is he correct? Explain.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">Yes, he is correct that 5.5 is a solution. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>5.5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is 27.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
